// The design dictionary. THE ONLY FILE ALLOWED TO HOLD A RAW VALUE.
//
// Document 13 section 2.1: appearance is DATA, in one place, and a view refers
// to a name and never to a number. Everything below is that one place, and
// `ui_guard.rs` is what keeps it that way.
//
// # The scales are CLOSED, and that is the point
//
// Section 2.3: a session that wants seven CANNOT HAVE IT - it takes the nearest
// named step. A table of values without a closed scale is not a dictionary, it
// is a register of excuses, because a new name may be added for every new
// number. So: adding a step here requires saying out loud why the existing ones
// did not fit, and that sentence belongs in the commit message.
//
// # Where the numbers came from
//
// The colours are not taste. Every foreground-background pair a screen may use
// is measured against WCAG contrast: seven foreground roles on three surfaces,
// 21 pairs, all above the 4.5 bar, the tightest being `text-muted` on
// `surface-raised` at 4.55. The table is not copied anywhere - it is produced
// by an internal script from THIS file, so a changed colour is re-measured
// rather than re-remembered. (Until 2026-09-14 this comment quoted "ten pairs,
// tightest 5.00" and pointed at ADR-4 for the table. No such table was ever
// written down, and 5.00 was the tightest pair on `surface` alone.)
//
// Those ratios hold against an OPAQUE background. D49 puts the resting-state
// alpha on the WINDOW'S BACKGROUND ONLY, never on the text, so letters always
// sit at full opacity. That is necessary but NOT sufficient: contrast is a
// ratio of two luminances, and a translucent background BLENDS with whatever
// window lies beneath the palette - most often a white form in a browser. The
// alpha below is therefore a measured floor, not a taste: at 0.90 every role
// the resting state shows (text-primary, text-secondary, accent, risk, ok)
// keeps 4.5 over a pure white backdrop with margin, on both renderers.
// `text-muted` does not - it would need 0.96 - so it MUST NOT appear in the
// resting state, and the same script checks the whole rule. D53.
//
// The alpha is honoured only when the system allows translucency at all: with
// transparency effects switched off (Windows), "reduce transparency" (macOS)
// or high contrast on (anywhere), the resting state is opaque. That rule lives
// in the application layer. This file only names the value.
//
// # One theme, and a second set of values that is not a theme
//
// D48: the product is DARK ONLY. No light theme, no switch, no following the
// system. That is a preference, and it is settled.
//
// High contrast is NOT a preference - it is an accessibility setting, switched
// on out of need. ADR-4 measured that no graphical toolkit consults it: not
// Slint, not the alternatives, and winit reads it solely to pick a title-bar
// theme. So it is ours to handle, and this dictionary will need a SECOND set of
// values beside the ordinary one. Second, not third: with one theme there is
// only one ordinary set to sit beside.
//
// Document 13 section 7 requires that second set to be a NEW FILE rather than a
// rewrite of the views, which is why nothing below is ever named by a screen -
// a screen picks a name, never a value.
//
// WHERE those values come from was measured on 2026-09-14 and settled in D53:
// Windows is the only system that offers COLOURS (eight system colours, every
// pair above 5.3:1 in all four shipped schemes), so there the second set is
// read from the system. macOS and Linux offer only a yes/no signal, so there
// the second set is our own, kept in that second file. Neither set is in this
// file yet - it arrives with the first screen.

// The typeface travels WITH the product. Measured, not assumed - see the
// `font-mono` entry at the bottom of this file for the numbers and for the
// sharp edge that comes with this import.
import "fonts/DejaVuSansMono.ttf";

export global Tokens {
    // ---- surfaces ----------------------------------------------------------
    out property <color> surface: #14161A;
    out property <color> surface-raised: #1C1F26;
    // The preview sits on a darker ground than the window so that a value made
    // of invisible characters still reads as an object with edges.
    out property <color> surface-sample: #0E1014;
    out property <color> border: #2A2F3A;
    // The edge of a control under the pointer. NOT a text colour and never a
    // background: it is measured as an edge, 1.56:1 against `border` so the
    // change is visible, and the keyboard focus (`accent`) still stands out
    // from it at 3.42:1 - document 13 and PORADNIK-GUI 10.10: a new state is
    // judged against the OLD state, not only against the ground.
    out property <color> border-strong: #454C5C;

    // ---- text --------------------------------------------------------------
    out property <color> text-primary: #E6E9EF;
    out property <color> text-secondary: #9AA4B4;
    out property <color> text-muted: #7E8797;

    // ---- meaning -----------------------------------------------------------
    // Document 13 section 9: meaning never hangs on colour alone. Each of these
    // is always accompanied by a shape or a label at the point of use. The
    // colour is the third carrier, never the only one.
    out property <color> accent: #7AA2F7;
    out property <color> risk: #E0AF68;
    out property <color> ok: #9ECE6A;
    // Stand-ins printed where an invisible character sits in a value.
    out property <color> invisible: #BB9AF7;

    // ---- spacing: a closed scale, six steps, nothing between them -----------
    out property <length> space-1: 4px;
    out property <length> space-2: 8px;
    out property <length> space-3: 12px;
    out property <length> space-4: 16px;
    out property <length> space-5: 24px;
    out property <length> space-6: 32px;

    // ---- type: sizes are steps, and they are PAIRED WITH A WEIGHT ----------
    // Section 2.3 asks for a fixed set of size-weight pairs rather than two
    // independent axes. The pairing is enforced one level up, by the text
    // components in ui/components/: a screen picks `Body`, never a size.
    out property <length> type-hint: 11px;
    out property <length> type-small: 12px;
    out property <length> type-body: 13px;
    out property <length> type-name: 15px;
    out property <length> type-preview: 17px;

    out property <int> weight-regular: 400;
    out property <int> weight-strong: 500;

    // ⚠️ Italic is deliberately absent, not forgotten. It measurably lowers
    // legibility and does so more sharply for dyslexic readers, so emphasis
    // goes through weight - document 13, section 2.3.

    // ---- window geometry ---------------------------------------------------
    // Named rather than written into the screen, because section 2.1 calls hard
    // geometry out separately: it does not merely drift, it BREAKS at another
    // system scale, another font size and a longer translation - and it breaks
    // on someone else's machine, never on the author's.
    out property <length> palette-width: 420px;
    out property <length> sample-height: 40px;

    // The pack window (step 7). An ordinary window the tester resizes, so the
    // default is where it opens and the minimum is where the layout still
    // reads: the query line, three rows of the list and the key hints. GUI
    // rules 21 and 22 - both are dimensions, and dimensions are tokens.
    out property <length> packs-width: 440px;
    out property <length> packs-height: 560px;
    out property <length> packs-min-width: 360px;
    out property <length> packs-min-height: 360px;
    // The thumb that says a list scrolls. Thin, because it is a sign rather
    // than a control - the list scrolls with the wheel and the arrow keys.
    out property <length> scroll-thumb: 4px;
    // The mark after the query that says where the next letter goes. Two
    // pixels rather than a hairline: it is the only sign of the keyboard INSIDE
    // the line, and one pixel of accent beside text reads as a stray stroke.
    out property <length> caret-width: 2px;

    // ---- opacity -----------------------------------------------------------
    // Background alpha of the palette in its resting state (ux-spec.md 2,
    // D49, D53). A measured floor, see the header: below 0.90 the roles shown
    // at rest lose 4.5:1 over a white window beneath. Applied to the surface
    // only, never to text, and only when the system permits translucency.
    out property <float> alpha-resting: 0.90;
    // A control that cannot be used. Faded as a whole, because "no invitation"
    // is the message (PORADNIK-GUI 10.9), and WCAG exempts an inactive control
    // from the contrast floor - it is not text the tester must read to act.
    out property <float> alpha-disabled: 0.5;

    // ---- shape -------------------------------------------------------------
    out property <length> radius-small: 4px;
    out property <length> radius: 6px;
    out property <length> radius-window: 10px;
    out property <length> hairline: 1px;

    // ---- the monospaced typeface -------------------------------------------
    //
    // DejaVu Sans Mono 2.37, shipped in ui/fonts/ and imported at the top of
    // this file. The name below must match the family the FILE declares, to the
    // character. `typeface.rs` reads the font's own name table and fails if the
    // two ever drift apart.
    //
    // # Why the product carries a font at all
    //
    // This used to say "Consolas", a family that exists only on Windows.
    // OBS-72 measured the consequence: on macOS and Linux the monospaced roles
    // silently became proportional, and monospace was picked for a reason - a
    // changing counter must not shift the characters around it.
    //
    // The obvious repair does not work, and that is measured too. Slint 1.17.1
    // does not resolve the generic CSS names: rendering the letter A produced
    // 448 inked pixels for the default face, 383 for "Consolas", 266 for
    // "Courier New" - and 448 for BOTH "monospace" and a deliberately invented
    // family name. A generic name behaves exactly like a name that does not
    // exist. `font_family` takes one family, and there is no fallback list to
    // write, so the only way to be sure of a face is to bring it.
    //
    // # Which one, and why this one
    //
    // Coverage was measured against what the catalogue's value column actually
    // demands - 180 code points needing a glyph, plus 12 the interface needs -
    // by reading each candidate's character map:
    //
    //     DejaVu Sans Mono 2.37     0.32 MB   151/180   12/12
    //     JetBrains Mono 2.304      0.26 MB   137/180   11/12
    //
    // JetBrains Mono is the smaller file and loses Arabic entirely, so it costs
    // 14 of the catalogue's code points to save 60 KB. Licence sieve, both
    // questions: (a) Bitstream Vera Fonts Copyright plus Arev - permissive and
    // GPL-3.0 compatible, and the rename clause is not engaged because the file
    // ships unmodified. (b) not applicable, a typeface is not a reference
    // dataset. The full text sits beside the font in ui/fonts/.
    //
    // 🔴 # What this import does NOT do
    //
    // It does not give the product a fallback chain. Measured with a positive
    // control, same binary, same container: with this kind of import in place
    // and no family named, 15 of the probe's code points had no glyph. Naming
    // the embedded family brought that to 8. Slint registers an imported font
    // as a NAMED FAMILY and never adds it to the fallback chain - unlike the
    // `SLINT_FONT_PATH` route, which does, and which takes a loose file beside
    // the binary rather than bytes inside it.
    //
    // So: for anything this face covers, the result is now the same on all
    // three systems. For anything it does not - the catalogue's Han, kana,
    // fullwidth forms, emoji and mathematical alphanumerics - the lottery is
    // exactly as it was, and measurably brutal: on Windows 4 of the probe's
    // code points had no glyph, in a container carrying only DejaVu it was 12,
    // and the two sets share ONE member. That gap belongs to the preview, it is
    // a decision rather than a defect, and it is open. OBS-66.
    out property <string> font-mono: "DejaVu Sans Mono";
}
